l from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='3a29077e-ab2e-4dba-8d19-51e15ed21d9e'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in the hotel reservation application, which consists of many microservices. So far, they\'ve checked the services, deployments, and pods, and noticed some pods have restarts. They checked the logs for \'geo\' and \'profile\' pods, which seemed normal, but the restarts are still a concern.\n\nNext, they should probably check other pods that have restarts. The next step is to look into the \'rate\' pod, which also has 3 restarts. Maybe there\'s a pattern here. The logs for \'geo\' and \'profile\' didn\'t show obvious errors, but maybe the \'rate\' pod has different issues. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they should check the metrics to see if there\'s any performance issues or errors being reported. Using the get_metrics tool with a specific query could help identify if there are any errors or high latency in certain services. For example, checking for error rates or failed requests in the \'rate\' service. \n\nBut since the user is focusing on pods with restarts, checking the next pod with restarts makes sense. The \'rate\' pod has 3 restarts, so checking its logs would be logical. If the logs show errors, that could indicate the root cause. If not, maybe moving to the \'recommendation\' or \'reservation\' pods. \n\nAlternatively, using get_dependency_graph could show if there are any dependency issues between services. If a service is failing because it can\'t connect to another service, that might show up in the dependency graph. But since the logs for \'geo\' and \'profile\' were normal, maybe the issue is elsewhere. \n\nWait, the user has already checked the logs for two pods. The next step is to check the \'rate\' pod. Let me check the previous steps again. The user checked \'geo\' (3 restarts), \'profile\' (2 restarts), then \'rate\' (3 restarts), \'recommendation\' (2 restarts), \'reservation\' (2 restarts), and \'user\' (2 restarts). \n\nSo, the next pod to check would be \'rate\' since it has 3 restarts. The user might need to check the logs for \'rate\' to see if there are any errors. If the logs are normal, maybe the issue is with the service\'s configuration or dependencies. \n\nAlternatively, using get_metrics to check for errors or failed requests in the \'rate\' service. For example, a query like "sum(rate(http_requests_total{job=\\"rate\\"} [5m]))" to see the number of requests, or "count by (status_code) (sum over time (http_requests_total{job=\\"rate\\"} ))" to check for 500 errors. \n\nBut the user\'s previous steps focused on logs. Maybe they should continue checking logs for other pods. Let\'s see, the next pod with restarts is \'rate\'. So the next tool call should be to get the logs for \'rate-6fff88f6f5-5pdc7\' to check for any errors. If that\'s normal, then maybe check the \'recommendation\' pod. \n\nAlternatively, if the logs don\'t show anything, maybe the issue is with the service\'s configuration, like a misconfigured environment variable or a missing dependency. But the user needs to check the logs first. \n\nSo, the next step is to check the \'rate\' pod\'s logs. If that\'s normal, then proceed to the next pod with restarts. If the logs show errors, that would point to the root cause. \n\nTherefore, the tool choice should be exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to get the logs for the \'rate\' pod.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The `profile` pod logs also appear normal, but it had 2 restarts. I\'ll now check the `rate` pod (with 3 restarts) to see if it has similar or different issues. This will help determine if the problem is isolated to specific services or part of a broader pattern.  \n**Target pod**: `rate-6fff88f6f5-5pdc7`', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 871, 'prompt_tokens': 7806, 'total_tokens': 8677, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cff32-8b11-76f3-b609-cac47e6d73e2-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 7806, 'output_tokens': 871, 'total_tokens': 8677}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='6b9d5941-7257-4b45-bba6-3188e37be9fc'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user is trying to diagnose issues in the hotel reservation application. So far, they've checked the pods for restarts and looked into the logs of geo and profile services, which seemed normal. Now they're moving to the rate pod, which has 3 restarts. The next step is to check the logs for the rate pod to see if there are any errors or issues causing the restarts.\n\nThe user is using the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool to get the logs. The previous steps showed that even though the pods had restarts, the logs didn't show obvious errors. But since the rate pod has the same pattern, checking its logs is logical. If the logs here also don't show issues, maybe there's a different problem, like resource constraints or network issues. Alternatively, maybe there's a configuration error that's not logged. \n\nWait, the user might need to check other aspects if the logs are clean. But according to the tools available, the next step is to get the logs for the rate pod. If that's normal, then maybe the issue is elsewhere, like a dependency or a service that's not responding. But with the current tools, checking logs is the next step. So the correct tool call is to get the logs for the rate pod.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs rate-6fff88f6f5-5pdc7 -n hotel-reservation"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bc8cd1ce244902eb', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 315, 'prompt_tokens': 8809, 'total_tokens': 9124, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cff32-b594-7930-9142-4534e7e55d2b-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl logs rate-6fff88f6f5-5pdc7 -n hotel-reservation'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bc8cd1ce244902eb', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 8809, 'output_tokens': 315, 'total_tokens': 9124}), ToolMessage(content='{"level":"info","time":"2026-03-18T04:25:31Z","message":"TLS disabled."}\n{"level":"info","time":"2026-03-18T04:25:31Z","message":"Set global log level: info"}\n{"level":"info","time":"2026-03-18T04:25:31Z","message":"Tune: setGCPercent to 100"}\n\x1b[90m2026-03-18T04:25:31Z\x1b[0m \x1b[32mINF\x1b[0m \x1b[1mcmd/rate/main.go:24\x1b[0m\x1b[36m >\x1b[0m \x1b[1mReading config...\x1b[0m\n\x1b[90m2026-03-18T04:25:31Z\x1b[0m \x1b[32mINF\x1b[0m \x1b[1mcmd/rate/main.go:37\x1b[0m\x1b[36m >\x1b[0m \x1b[1mRead database URL: mongodb-rate:27017\x1b[0m\n\x1b[90m2026-03-18T04:25:31Z\x1b[0m \x1b[32mINF\x1b[0m \x1b[1mcmd/rate/main.go:38\x1b[0m\x1b[36m >\x1b[0m \x1b[1mInitializing DB connection...\x1b[0m\n\x1b[90m2026-03-18T04:25:31Z\x1b[0m \x1b[32mINF\x1b[0m \x1b[1mcmd/rate/db.go:39\x1b[0m\x1b[36m >\x1b[0m \x1b[1mNew session successfull...\x1b[0m\n\x1b[90m2026-03-18T04:25:31Z\x1b[0m \x1b[32mINF\x1b[0m \x1b[1mcmd/rate/db.go:41\x1b[0m\x1b[36m >\x1b[0m \x1b[1mGenerating test data...\x1b[0m\n\x1b[90m2026-03-18T04:25:31Z\x1b[0m \x1b[32mINF\x1b[0m \x1b[1mcmd/rate/main.go:41\x1b[0m\x1b[36m >\x1b[0m \x1b[1mSuccessfull\x1b[0m\n\x1b[90m2026-03-18T04:25:31Z\x1b[0m \x1b[32mINF