since the user already has some pods with restarts, focusing on metrics and traces might help identify if there\'s a common issue, like a failing service that others depend on.\n\nWait, the \'geo\' pod\'s logs didn\'t show any errors, but it had 3 restarts. Maybe the issue is not in the pod itself but in the services it depends on. For example, if the MongoDB instance for geo is down, that could cause the geo service to fail. So checking the status of the MongoDB pods or the service might be necessary. But the user hasn\'t checked that yet.\n\nAlternatively, using \'get_operations\' from Grafana to see if there are any operations that are failing for the \'geo\' service. Or checking the metrics for the \'geo\' service to see if there are any high error rates or latency spikes. The \'get_metrics\' tool could be used with a query like \'sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="geo"}[5m]))\' to see the number of requests, or \'count by (status_code) (sum over time (http_requests_total{job="geo"}))\' to check for error codes.\n\nBut the user might need to first check if there are any metrics available for the services. Since the application uses Go and gRPC, maybe the metrics are exposed via Prometheus. So using \'get_metrics\' with appropriate queries could reveal if there are any issues. Alternatively, checking the logs of other pods with restarts to see if they have similar issues. For example, the \'profile\' pod had 2 restarts. Checking its logs might show if there\'s a different error.\n\nBut the user can only make a certain number of tool calls. So the next logical step is to check the logs of another pod with restarts, like \'profile\', to see if there are any errors. If that\'s not possible, maybe check the metrics for the \'geo\' service to see if there\'s a pattern. Alternatively, using \'get_traces\' to see if there are any traces that are failing or taking too long.\n\nWait, the user already used \'get_traces\' in a previous step? No, in the initial steps, they us