starts). \n\nSo, the next pod to check would be \'rate\' since it has 3 restarts. The user might need to check the logs for \'rate\' to see if there are any errors. If the logs are normal, maybe the issue is with the service\'s configuration or dependencies. \n\nAlternatively, using get_metrics to check for errors or failed requests in the \'rate\' service. For example, a query like "sum(rate(http_requests_total{job=\\"rate\\"} [5m]))" to see the number of requests, or "count by (status_code) (sum over time (http_requests_total{job=\\"rate\\"} ))" to check for 500 errors. \n\nBut the user\'s previous steps focused on logs. Maybe they should continue checking logs for other pods. Let\'s see, the next pod with restarts is \'rate\'. So the next tool call should be to get the logs for \'rate-6fff88f6f5-5pdc7\' to check for any errors. If that\'s normal, then maybe check the \'recommendation\' pod. \n\nAlternatively, if the logs don\'t show anything, maybe the issue is with the service\'s configuration, like a misconfigured environment variable or a missing dependency. But the user needs to check the logs first. \n\nSo, the next step is to check the \'rate\' pod\'s logs. If that\'s normal, then proceed to the next pod with restarts. If the logs show errors, that would point to the root cause. \n\nTherefore, the tool choice should be exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to get the logs for the \'rate\' pod.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nreasoning: The `profile` pod logs also appear normal, but it had 2 restarts. I\'ll now check the `rate` pod (with 3 restarts) to see if it has similar or different issues. This will help determine if the problem is isolated to specific services or part of a broader pattern.  \n**Target pod**: `rate-6fff88f6f5-5pdc7`', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 871, 'prompt_tokens': 7806, 'total_tokens': 8677, 'completion_tokens_